1445. § 1 . The Supreme Court of the Apostolic Signature judges:
1 complaints for annulment and requests for restitution in integrum and other appeals against rotational sentences;
2 appeals in cases regarding the status of persons whom the Roman Rota refuses to admit for new examination;
3 the exceptions of suspicion and other causes against the Auditors of the Roman Rota for acts carried out in the exercise of their function;
4 the conflicts of jurisdiction referred to in c. 1416.
§ 2 . This same Court settles disputes arising from an act of ecclesiastical administrative power that are legitimately brought to it, as well as other administrative controversies that have been referred to it by the Roman Pontiff or by the dicasteries of the Roman Curia, and conflicts of jurisdiction between said dicasteries.
§ 3 . It also corresponds to this Supreme Court:
1 monitor the proper administration of justice and determine that proceedings be taken against lawyers or attorneys, if necessary;
2 extend the jurisdiction of the courts;
3 promote and approve the erection of the courts referred to in the ccs. 1423 and 1439.

1504. The statement of claim must:
1 specify before which judge the case is brought, what is requested and against whom;
2 indicate what law the actor relies on and, at least in general terms, what facts and evidence he relies on to prove what he claims;
3 be signed by the plaintiff or his attorney, indicating the day, month and year, as well as the place where they live or say they have residence for the purposes of receiving documents;
4 indicate the domicile or quasi-domicile of the defendant.

1505. § 1 . The single judge or the president of the collegiate court, after verifying that the matter is within his jurisdiction and that the plaintiff has the legal capacity to act in court, must admit or reject the document of claim as soon as possible, by decree.
§ 2 . The statement of claim can only be rejected:
1 if the judge or court is incompetent;
2 if it is certain that the actor lacks procedural capacity;
3 if what is mandated by c. 1504, 1-3;
4 if from the same document of claim it is deduced with certainty that the petition lacks any foundation and that no foundation can be expected to emerge from the process.
§ 3 . If the document has been rejected due to defects that can be corrected, the plaintiff can present a new, correctly written one before the same judge.
§ 4 . Within the useful period of ten days, the party may file a reasoned appeal against the rejection of the writing before the court of appeal, or before the college if it was rejected by the president; and the question of rejection must be decided as quickly as possible.

1512. Once the summons has been legitimately notified or the parties have appeared before the judge to discuss the case:
1 the thing ceases to be whole;
2 the case becomes the responsibility of that judge or the court before which the action has been brought, as long as they are competent;
3 the jurisdiction of the delegated judge is consolidated, in such a way that it is not extinguished when the right of the delegated judge ceases;
4 the prescription is interrupted, if nothing else has been established;
5 the lis pendens begins, and, therefore, the principle "while the litigation is pending, nothing should be innovated" is immediately applied.

1518. When a litigant dies, or changes state, or ceases in the office for which he acts:
1 if the case has not yet concluded, the instance is suspended until it is resumed by the heir of the deceased or his successor or the legitimately interested party;
2 if the case is concluded, the judge must continue it, summoning the attorney; and if there is not one, to the heir of the deceased or his successor.

1572. When evaluating the testimonies, the judge must consider the following aspects, requesting testimonial letters, if necessary:
1 what is the condition of the person and their honesty;
2 if he declares on his own knowledge, mainly what he has seen or heard, or if he expresses his opinion, or what is common feeling or what he has heard from others;
3 whether the witness is constantly and firmly consistent with himself, or whether he is variable, uncertain or hesitant;
4 if there are competing testimonies, or if the statement is confirmed or not with other elements of evidence.

1594. If on the day and time set for the response, the plaintiff does not appear or provide an adequate excuse:
1 the judge will summon him again;
2 if the actor does not obey this new summons, it is presumed that he renounces the instance, according to ccs. 1524-1525;
3 if you later wish to intervene in the process, comply with the provisions of c. 1593.

1600. § 1 . After the conclusion of the case, the judge may call the same or other witnesses, or order that tests not previously requested be performed, only:
1 in cases in which only the private good of the parties is involved, if all of them agree;
2 in other cases, after hearing the parties and provided there is a serious reason and any danger of fraud or bribery is avoided;
3 in all cases, when it is probable that, if new evidence is not admitted, the sentence will be unjust, for the reasons set forth in c.

1611. The sentence must:
1 settle the controversy discussed before the court, giving each doubt the appropriate answer;
2 determine what the obligations of the parties derived from the trial are, and how they are to be fulfilled;
3 present the reasons or motives, both legal and factual, on which the operative part of the sentence is based;
4 determine what refers to the costs of the litigation.

1620. The sentence suffers from an irremediable defect of nullity if:
1 was ruled by a completely incompetent judge;
2 was issued by someone who lacks the power to judge in the court before which the case has been tried;
3 the judge issued the sentence under duress due to violence or serious fear;
4 the trial has been carried out without the judicial request referred to in c. 1501, or was not filed against any defendant;
5 occurred between parties of which at least one does not have the capacity to act in court;
6 someone acted on behalf of another without a legitimate mandate;
7 one of the two parties was denied the right of defense;
8 did not settle the controversy, not even partially.
1620. can be proposed perpetually as an exception and as an action, within a period of ten years from the date of the sentence, before the judge who issued it.

1629. There is no appeal:
1 against the sentence of the Supreme Pontiff himself or of the Apostolic Signature;
2 against the sentence that suffers from a defect of nullity, unless the appeal is joined with the complaint for nullity, in accordance with c. 1625;
3 against the sentence that has become res judicata;
4 against the judge's decree or interlocutory sentence that does not have the force of a final sentence, unless it is combined with the appeal against the final sentence;
5 against the sentence or decree in a case that according to the law must be resolved as quickly as possible.

1641. Remaining safe from what is prescribed in c. 1643, res judicata occurs:
1 if there are two conforming rulings between the same litigants, on the same request made for the same reasons;
2 if an appeal against the sentence had not been filed within the useful period;
3 if, at the level of appeal, the instance had expired or had been waived;
4 if a final sentence was handed down, against which there is no appeal, in accordance with c. 1629.

1649. § 1 . The Bishop, who is responsible for moderating the tribunal, must dictate norms regarding:
1 the order of the parties to pay or compensate legal costs;
2 the fees of attorneys, lawyers, experts and interpreters, as well as compensation for witnesses;
3 the granting of free sponsorship or reduced costs;
4 the compensation for damages owed by the person who not only lost the lawsuit, but also litigated recklessly;
5 the deposit of money or guarantee that must be provided for the payment of costs and compensation for damages.
§ 2 . There is no separate appeal against the ruling on costs, fees and compensation for damages, but the party may appeal within fifteen days, before the same judge, who may modify the assessment.

1720. If the Ordinary deems that it should be proceeded by extrajudicial decree:
1 will inform the accused of the accusation and the evidence, giving him the opportunity to defend himself, unless the accused, legitimately summoned, does not wish to appear;
2 must carefully weigh all evidence and arguments with two advisors;
3 if the crime is established with certainty and the criminal action has not been extinguished, a decree will be issued in accordance with cc. 1342-1350, setting out, at least briefly, the legal and factual reasons.

1745. But if the parish priest challenges the alleged cause and its reasons, alleging reasons that the Bishop considers insufficient, he, in order to act validly:
1 will invite the parish priest so that, once the file has been examined, he presents his objections in writing and provides evidence to the contrary, if he has any;
2 after this and completing the file if necessary, he will study the matter with the parish priests referred to in c.
